Where the protocol comes from
-----------------------------

This program speaks to the same hardware as ykushcmd, the control application
Yepkit publishes for their YKUSH boards:

    https://github.com/Yepkit/ykush
    Copyright 2017 Yepkit Lda (www.yepkit.com)

What was taken from it is functional: the USB control protocol — command
opcodes, report layout, the meaning of the answers — and the names of the
command line switches. Facts and interfaces of that kind are not what copyright
protects, so this notice is here because the work deserves crediting, not
because a license compels it.

What was not taken: any source file, any line of the help text, any message the
program prints. Help and output of this program are written from scratch and
were checked against every string literal in ykush_help.cpp, ykush3.cpp and
yk_usb_device.cpp — nothing overlaps.

Yepkit Lda has neither endorsed nor reviewed this work and does not support it.
Their trademarks and product names are used only to say which hardware this
program drives.

The behaviour differs from the original in these points, which may matter when
switching over:

  - only the YKUSH3 board is covered; YKUSH, YKUSH2 and YKUSHXS are not
  - the messages are worded differently, so a script that parses the output of
    the original has to be adapted
  - a command the board rejects exits with 1; the original never reads the
    answer and exits with 0
  - the state of the external 5V port is decoded, which the original omits
  - the length of an I2C read is transmitted as a single byte, correcting a
    conversion in the original that corrupts the report for ten bytes and more
  - a GPIO read prints its value instead of only setting the exit code
  - control characters in a device supplied serial number are replaced before
    the string reaches the terminal

The full and current list of deliberate behavioural differences is the table
"Where it differs from the C++ original" in the README.


